A||N||37||Zorba||V8 - How much flex with Strut Rod?||||1||
Z||000000||Zorba||12-12-2000||10:23 PM||||Upon revving, my new v8 fiero engine moves about ~1/4" or so back and forth with the torque. Is this normal. How much flex should the engine have with revs? Is 1/4" acceptable or too much. Is it supposed not to have any flex at all? Sorry for the dumb question but I haven't seen this covered anywhere in the forum, kits or tapes.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fsrowsell||12-12-2000||11:09 PM||||Zorba, I used two strut rods for that very same reason. I put one at each end of the right head. My engine was torqueing too much and one day under a heavy foot, the front pulley touched the frame rail and caused my W/P pulley shaft to break. But with the two rods the engine did not move at all... <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zorba:<BR><B>Upon revving, my new v8 fiero engine moves about ~1/4" or so back and forth with the torque. Is this normal. How much flex should the engine have with revs? Is 1/4" acceptable or too much. Is it supposed not to have any flex at all? Sorry for the dumb question but I haven't seen this covered anywhere in the forum, kits or tapes.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||12-12-2000||11:15 PM||||With my 3800 SC, it moves A LOT. It moves at least an inch left to right. The alternator pulley touches the strut tower on left turns.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RNGCRSH||12-13-2000||11:40 AM||||TerryK:<P>sounds like you have bad mounts or cradle bushings 1" of side to side on a transverse mount engine is way to much.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||12-13-2000||05:29 PM||||Nope, all new. Too much torque.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>You have to be really wailing on it on a left turn and then it will swing over. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tina||12-13-2000||11:09 PM||||Yep, definitly upgrade to two, three if possible. That is if you drive like me.<BR>Broke the bolt where the torque strut bolts up 3 times this summer before I smarted up.<P>Was worth it though everytime.<P>Tina<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||12-14-2000||11:57 AM||||I'm running a single dogbone with my V8 Fiero and it ain't enough. Going to add a torque strut to the other end of the motor this spring. My 1998 Grand Prix GT with a 3800 V6 uses two dogbones from the factory.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Zorba||12-14-2000||04:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by batboy:<BR><B>I'm running a single dogbone with my V8 Fiero and it ain't enough. Going to add a torque strut to the other end of the motor this spring. My 1998 Grand Prix GT with a 3800 V6 uses two dogbones from the factory.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think you can make a strut for less than<BR>$10 with parts from the hardware store. A threaded pipe and two ends with holes in them for the bolt. They are adjustable also. That's what I am doing for my dog bone replacement. I already have the strut. I double welded the base of it on the cradle to be safe.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||12-14-2000||09:02 PM||||Since I can afford it, I'm probably going to spend a little extra and get a couple aircraft quality heim joints for the ends of my torque strut.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tina||12-14-2000||09:17 PM||||Batboy,care to explain what a heim joint is?<BR>Please.<P>BTW<BR>Asked my one man pitccrew about the flex.<BR>He couldn'd find that topic covered either.<BR>His opinon though, the less movement by the engine ,the better it is.<P>Tina||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||12-14-2000||09:30 PM||||Heim joints are also known as spherical rod end bearings or ball joint rod ends. They fit the end of a threaded shaft (male or female) and have a ball that swivels to prevent binding if there is any movement. They are commonly used for suspension components and steering linkages for all kinds of race and off-road vehicles. Almost like a Fiero dogbone with a ball swivel instead of the rubber.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tina||12-14-2000||09:45 PM||||I think I can hear a faint <BR>Me want me want from the garage.<BR>Funny,the only thing that's in there is my little red Fiero.<P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||12-14-2000||10:13 PM||||Wow, Rocky explained it better than I could. If you look at this pic of traction bars (which have nothing to do with Fieros), check out the chrome rods at the top with the spherical ends (or heim joints). Basically, that's what my torque bar will look like (minus the rest of the traction bars shown in the pic).<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.jegs.com/photos/62020189.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Will||12-15-2000||02:57 PM||||1/4" inch just on revving, without any load is probably too much.<P>The real test is to take the deck lid off, install a helper in the passenger seat and go screw around in an empty parking lot. Have the helper turn and watch the engine as you accelerate, decelerate and turn. Watching how the engine moves will help you decide exactly how to deal with it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||12-15-2000||04:10 PM||||I'd rather put my helper in the trunk...a real good viewfrom there...hahaha.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Zorba||12-15-2000||05:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by batboy:<BR><B>I'd rather put my helper in the trunk...a real good viewfrom there...hahaha.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have a friend who's a midget and he can watch the movement from the trunk with the decklid off. I am sure I would get some stares.<P>||||reg||6
Z||000016||sluppy123||12-15-2000||05:26 PM||||If anyone wants to have a custom aluminum strut built I'd be willing to do it. I recently bought a Vertical Milling machine and can make just about any size of strut. I'd prefer to use alum. so they can be all one piece. all I would need is the Dimensions for the srtut and a little time.  <P>------------------<BR>-Brian S. <BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle||||reg||1
Z||000017||Tina||12-16-2000||12:00 PM||||Must of been the idea of a shiny new part for my Fiero, or lack of coffee.<BR>How exactly would you bolt it on?<BR>You would have to make a bracket for the block it self, right?<BR>On the bottom it might work with out a bracket though.At least I think,let me get some more coffee.<BR>Sluppy 123,nice thought. Aluminum isn't strong enough.But, he I've been wrong before.<BR>Somebody in the trunk?<BR>People won't even get in the passeger seat with me behind the wheel.<P>Tina<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||sluppy123||12-16-2000||01:01 PM||||"Aluminum isn't strong enough"- Haha now that's funny, was that a joke? Aluminum is a very "strong" material, A good grade of alloyed alum has a very high strength to weight ratio, far exceeding Most other materials. Plus it's corrosion resistant and can be shined real nice. The fiero's original dogbone is made out of cast alum and they hold up just fine. <P>So unless your running a engine that makes enough torque to create a 25,000+ lbs pull on the dogbone, alum will work just fine.<P>------------------<BR>-Brian S. <BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle||||reg||3
Z||000019||Archie||12-16-2000||03:04 PM||||Why make one when you can buy one for less $$.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/67.jpg"> <P>As you can see it's mounted to the end of the cylinder head and goes down and back a little to mount a small piece of angle iron welded to the cradle. Adjust the Torque Rod so that it neither raises or pulls down on the near-by transmission mount. The Torque Rod is meant to control engine movement keeping the engine/trans. mounts In a "status quo" position. it is not meant to change positioning of any of the items that we have mounted so far. Note that this A-1 Racing porducts strut can be purchased at most local speed shops for &lt;$50. It is made of AL, However it is mounted at a 90 degree angle to the axis of the engine crankshaft, thus taking the load thru the length of the strut. It is also mounted on the back side of the engine. A SBC engine turns clockwise as viewed from the front, thus the torque of the engine forces the engine CCW. With the strut mounted on the back side of the engine the torque is "pushing down" on the strut, which is preferrable to mounting it on the front side of the engine where it will "pull" on the strut, in this caes the strut will not be any stronger than the bolts holding it in place. <P>Mario, to answer your question.... that engine should move no more than 1/8" North and South and "0" left to right. If it's moving more than that, you've got something done incorrectly.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||12-17-2000||12:57 AM||||Tina, yep, that's how I'm going to mount my torque strut, just like Archie's. Tina, I'm sure you've noticed if you really nail the throttle on the open road, the rear of the car will twitch. With my 350 V8 it's quite noticeable. This is torque steer and the engine and cradle moving around under load causes most of that problem. I'm hoping by adding a torque rod to help the existing dogbone and then using poly cradle bushings (many people use metal cradle bushings) that I can significantly reduce my torque steer problem.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Archie||12-17-2000||03:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by batboy:<BR><B>I'm sure you've noticed if you really nail the throttle on the open road, the rear of the car will twitch. With my 350 V8 it's quite noticeable. This is torque steer </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>BatBoy, Do you have poly bushings in the rear "A" arms?<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000022||batboy||12-17-2000||10:27 AM||||Archie, yes I have poly bushings in all the control arms and on the front sway bar. However, my rear sway bar is still in it's box and uninstalled. The control arm poly bushings and the rear coil-over kit really helped with the torque steer, but I'm getting visible movement at the rubber cradle bushings.<P>The Batmobile is put up for the winter right now (no insurance), but next spring I'm swapping out the stocker oil burning truck 350 that's currently in it (I started calling it "Ol' Smokey") for a fresh 1988 Z-28 roller 350, so I'm planning on doing new poly cradle bushings and the torque rod at that time.||||reg||1
Z||000023||batboy||12-17-2000||10:38 AM||||Tina, you are right to be very cautious about using aluminum in high strength situations. However, there are now plenty of space age aluminum alloys that are super strong, by weight even stronger than steel. If you use billet or extruded high strength aluminum, it should work quite nicely.<P>Sluppy bro, if you can build a nice looking, strong, inexpensive torque strut for these V8 Fieros, I'll buy one from you in a heartbeat. For the newer Forum members that might not know, I admire Sluppy for building a beautiful pair of Fieros as seen below. Sluppy, I still have those front air dam instuctions you made for me. One of these days I'm actually going to install that chin spoiler.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/blkwht2.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||Zorba||12-17-2000||09:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>Why make one when you can buy one for less $$.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/67.jpg">  <P>As you  <BR>Archie</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Gentlemen:<P>If the strut rod is not adjusted properly, one end of it can come off with a high torque engine. That's what mine did and it stripped the threads on the shaft, hence the problem (Wobble). I have acquired another strut rod and now the engine barely moves under load. Problem solved. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Tina||12-17-2000||10:13 PM||||Batboy, could you send me pic of the dogbone<BR>in your fiero hooked up to the 350<P>Thanks<BR>Tina<BR>||||reg||1
