Z||000036||batboy||12-19-2000||08:43 PM||||Tina, my advice to you is not to do anymore "burnouts" with your V8 Fiero. Sooner or later, you will suffer another tranny failure. What I do is to launch and only chip the tires. After it hooks up good, then I nail the throttle. Besides, spinning the tires means you're losing traction and losing ET and probably losing the race. That's a lot of weight and torque sitting on top of those drive wheels, not to mention you have fairly wide tires. I know you're young...I used to like to do smokey burnout when I was a young too, until I tore out the rear end ring and pinion gears and shelled out a Muncie 4 speed in my ol' 68 Chevelle SS 396. Yep, I was showing off. Expensive lesson too.<P>I've used that air cleaner on the Batmobile for a year and half now, plus it was on the car for another year prior to me owning it. The only way it could burn up is if the engine backfired through the carb. I ran one of those air cleaners on a GTO for a couple of years too. Like I said though, I'm open to suggestions for one that is better.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/batkid3.gif">   <B>BATBOY</B><BR>1985 SE auto with 350 Chevy V8 (Batmobile),<BR>suspension mods, lowered, GA brakes, etc...||||reg||1
